:"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-27T04:53:04-07:00","id":1410655,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Accelerator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/223770-homebrews-of-the-first-circle?p=1405439#post1405439\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh. Is he a 1st circle demon?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yes, though I used the blood apes as a guidepost, so they're on the stronger end for 1st circle demons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my RPG podcast: <a href=\"http://thestorytold.libsyn.com\" target=\"_blank\">The Story Told</a><br /> Follow my ongoing Exalted Campaign: <a href=\"https://thefallofjiara.obsidianportal.com/adventure-log\" target=\"_blank\">The Fall of Jiara</a><br /> Check out my Community Content: <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse.php?author=Chazz%20Kellner\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse....hazz%20Kellner</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/702-elfspeaker","name":"ElfSpeaker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar702_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-10T12:06:48-07:00","id":1412874,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forums.sufficientvelocity.com/threads/ascensions-and-transgressions-the-tales-of-keris-dulmeadokht-exalted-game.32784/page-111#post-11512151\" target=\"_blank\">https://forums.sufficientvelocity.co...#post-11512151</a><br /> <br /> <b><i>Progeny of the Midnight Whisper</i></b><br /> <b>Alomlos</b><br /> <br /> A fully grown man; his back straight and his posture proud, reaches only up to the shoulder of an alomlo; its back and crest rising high above his head like a mountain of muscle. The eight insectoid eyes of a jumping spider are arrayed unblinking across its equine face, its bristly hair is dark and oily, and moonlight casts its shadow as that of a gargantuan, monstrous spider. The size of the martyr drays is no lie; for they are frighteningly strong, able to do the work of ten mortal horses with ease.<br /> <br /> Deeply compassionate creatures - though none too bright - alomlos see their great strength as a great responsibility. With their powerful stride they will wander from settlement to settlement, and in their deep rumbling voices ask if there is any labour they can spare the weak and fragile by taking it upon their own mighty backs. Upon finding something they can ply their muscle to, they will throw themselves into the task with a fearsome passion; hauling cartloads of stone or dragging ploughs through soil as hard as stone.<br /> <br /> The work ethic of a martyr dray is ardent to the point of self-destruction. Alomlos will toil without rest for days until they collapse from the pain of torn muscles and exhaustion, or apply themselves to tasks even their prodigious strength cannot stand up to. Their blood is sticky tar, and when they overwork themselves it bleeds from their skin and drips in globules from their glistening coats. Often they must be talked into pausing to eat and sleep - and the greater the need or weakness of those they work for, the more effort they will put into their labour. Those who know them well in the demonic realm from whence they hail know to tether them with ropes of spider silk for their own good; the only substance they will not strain against and tear in their haste to return to their tasks.<br /> <br /> Sorcerers invoke the martyr drays for their strength, and also at times for the potent adhesive that can be harvested from their flanks. They are poor warhorses compared to their farisyya cousins, but they tower above even mounted cataphracts and are unmatched in sheer brute force and stamina. While they will not resist a binding if told there is work for them that will aid those weaker, the wise sorcerer keeps a close eye on his herd - or else he will return to find they have worked themselves to debility in his absence. When the last horse of a village in desperate need of labour dies, its shadow may twist into that of a spider at the first touch of the moon\u2019s light as an alomlo steps into Creation to offer its service.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","user":"Accelerator","date":". 09-10-2020, 05:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-10T13:09:30-07:00","id":1412882,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://forums.sufficientvelocity.com/threads/ascensions-and-transgressions-the-tales-of-keris-dulmeadokht-exalted-game.32784/page-57#post-7535043\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forums.sufficientvelocity.com/data/svg/20/1/1794123741/logo_icon.png\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forums.sufficientvelocity.com/threads/ascensions-and-transgressions-the-tales-of-keris-dulmeadokht-exalted-game.32784/page-57#post-7535043\" target=\"_blank\"> Ascensions and Transgressions: the Tales of Keris Dulmeadokht (Exalted game) </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forums.sufficientvelocity.com/threads/ascensions-and-transgressions-the-tales-of-keris-dulmeadokht-exalted-game.32784/page-57#post-7535043\" target=\"_blank\"> https://forums.sufficientvelocity.com/threads/ascensions-and-transgressions-the-tales-of-keris-dulmeadokht-exalted-game.32784/page-57#post-7535043 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> So, this is a thread for Ascensions and Transgressions; my 1-on-1 Exalted game with... </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-10T14:31:25-07:00","id":1412903,"title":"","content":"<i><b>Alomlos, the Shadow Steeds</b></i><br /> <br /> Progeny of the Midnight Whisper<br /> An alomlo is a strange steed, and no rider has ever mounted one. They have no form of their own - no back to carry a passenger, no hooves to bear them onward, no mouth to take a bridle. And yet they have reflections of all these things, for they are seen in the shadows of their hosts. When an alomlo slips into the shadow of a host - be they human, demon or even animal - the creature's shadow warps and twists into a new form; a horse with eight hoofed spider's legs and a mane of midnight veils. This is the only sign of the alomlo, who otherwise rides its host with utmost subtlety and steers them away from bright lights that would betray its presence.<br /> <br /> The preferred steed of an alomlo is a creature in pain. By day they will numb the aches or agonies of their hosts, and by night they will draw them into a pool of deep shadows, where the anchor of their body is lost in pleasant dreams. Here, a host may well be aware of the shadow steed - often in their happiest moments they will find themselves riding a flickering mass of shadows, or feel an enormous spider's leg brush affectionately against their back. Those who ride an alomlo in their dreams are utterly numb to all pain, kept insensate to anything that ails them and unable to lift so much as a finger. In a truly dire situation, the steed can pull their host deeper still into their shadows, carrying them to another patch of darkness. They are loathe to do so, as such intimate contact with the shadows risks a host being lost forever; never to wake from slumber.<br /> <br /> Emotional pain confuses a shadow steed. Being creatures without form, they are gifted with a paradoxical intuition about physical maladies, but more ephemeral hurts are foreign to them. An alomlo who encounters a tearful child or a traumatised soldier may as easily dismiss them as attempt to find the source of their suffering. Should they choose the latter, it is unlikely they will ever let their host go, for while bones heal and cuts close, a broken heart may always linger. The alomlo might tug their patient into dreams every night, never understanding why their attempts at aid make matters worse.<br /> <br /> Sorcerers summon alomlos to bring pleasant dreams and ward off nightmares, as well as to provide anaesthesia to patients in pain. They cannot operate themselves, but their medical intuition is rarely wrong, and from their place within a creature's shadow they can give advice and suggest what ails their host. Immaterial creatures of darkness, they gain a point of Limit every hour they are exposed to light without a host to hide behind. Alomlos can be summoned without sorcery when silent tears mix with blood under the absent light of a new moon.<br /> <br /> <i><b>Oovuloo, the Tar Balls</b></i><br /> <br /> Progeny of the Midnight Whisper<br /> Thought is not a great strength of the ooluvoo. They are simple creatures who, by and large, believe what they are told. Only in one thing are they guarded against trickery and befuddlement, and this is their duty. An ooluvo is compelled to defend; to protect. If told that something or someone is in need of their service, they will guard them infallibly; ignoring all orders to stop save those of their charge. Neither boredom nor distraction plague them, for they lack the curiosity to seek more stimulation than the eternal watch for threats.<br /> <br /> An oovulo in its ground state resembles a ball of tar the size of a human torso; warm to the touch and with a thick and rubbery surface, which moves by rolling itself along the ground - even uphill, should it choose. Tar-balls are amorphous beings that can shape themselves into walls or shields, extruding tentacles with which to act, move or block. They are skilled defensive fighters who can turn aside every blow from a flurry of swordstrokes, though they possess no similar ability in offense. Some oovuloo possess multiple charges, or must protect their principal from many threats. In these situations they can tear themselves in two; splitting into two smaller, weaker bodies that can each divide once more in turn. Though these lesser forms are much weaker and less potent than the oovulo\u2019s combined state, they are no less dedicated to their duty, and will recombine when the fight is over.<br /> <br /> In one arena the oovuloo are almost powerless, and this is the cold. Their liquid bodies freeze easily, and low temperatures hinder their movement and dampen their intellect yet further. When puddles freeze over and frost forms on buildings, tar-balls are slowed to a crawl; their bodies locking up and losing their amorphous nature. Much colder and they freeze over entirely, and risk being shattered - for they are no less determined to put themselves between their charges and harm in this state. While freezing and even shattering will not necessarily kill a tar-ball outright, it is harmful nonetheless, and if left too long in many pieces they will lose the ability to recombine and die. Some of the tattered globs left behind may eventually form into new oovuloo, should they be fed tar and kept out of the light of the sun and moon.<br /> <br /> Sorcerers summon the oovuloo as bodyguards and defensive soldiers - for they have few other uses, and are certainly not scintillating conversationalists. They rarely resist binding, and often do not even require it, for telling them who or what they are to protect is generally enough to convince them. Those depending on them to keep interlopers out of a treasury must be wary, of course, of those wise to their nature. A clever thief would not be able to convince an oovulo that they were to guard something other than the treasury they had been set in front of, nor that he was an exception to the rules of &quot;nobody but the owner may enter&quot;, but they would be easily persuaded that a cunning thief was already within and that they must rush to find her. Oovulo take a point of Limit whenever their charge is injured or otherwise attacked, and may escape into Creation when strands of tar are caught between an attacker\u2019s blow and their victim at night.<br /> <br /> <i><b>Ruhazae, the Raiment Skins</b></i><br /> <br /> Progeny of the Midnight Whisper<br /> When Calesco weaves one of the ruhazae, she uses her elder sister\u2019s offcast ribbons and her own shadowy hair. In its weaving the demon experiences terrifying dreams of the Silent Wind, who sired both Echo and Calesco. For that reason, the raiment skins fear Adorjan as strongly as any demon that dwells within Malfeas, and they hate silence above all things. They tremble constantly, rustling like fine satin or silk and sing in piping voices outside the range of men, though bats and mice and other such creatures can hear them and dance when they are near.<br /> <br /> The ruhazae have the form of sheer cloth, in red, white and black. Hundreds and hundreds of eyes are woven upon the fabric, and these eyes blink and watch for soundless wind. When they must move, they spin around in circles with great speed until their cloth takes the form of insect wings and then they fly with a buzzing rattling. However, they prefer to hide from Adorjan upon the form of another. A ruhazae pretends to be fine sheer clothing and drapes itself over a living being, contorting itself to hug the body tight. It prefers nothing more than for its host to cover it up with other clothing, for then it will not be found.<br /> <br /> Alas, when it is exposed the certainty that Adorjan is coming for it strikes the demon. To defend itself, therefore, it spins hair-thin illusions around its wearer so all remember their beauty, but none recall their semblance. To a Southerner, they resemble a tantalising beauty of the South; to a Westerner they have the seeming of a foam-decked fantasy of a sailor; to a demon of Hell they look like a neomah or other demon they lust after. The beautiful lies of the Midnight Whisper fog the minds of onlookers so such things appear self-evident. Only one who is not stirred by such beauty, or who desires truth over love, can see through these lies - and then they see the rustling cloth staring at them with absolute terror.<br /> <br /> To a sorcerer, the terror of the ruhazae is a means of disguise and obfuscation. When wearing one, they might walk through a town without anyone recalling their face. Alas, the ruhazae and the peronelles hate each other, for both desire to cling to their wearer\u2019s skin, and so they will fight like cats and dogs. Silence is anathema to these demons, and they take one point of Limit per action they are forced to be silent. A ruhazae can crawl from its home realm when the shame and fear of someone unrighteously exposed in the eyes of others sings its siren call, and the demon responds to provide both clothing and escape.<br /> <br /> <i><b>Stilicustodes, the Starlit Sentries</b></i><br /> <br /> <br /> Progeny of the Midnight Whisper<br /> Pass four fingers across the eye and look just to the left of centre. There! The faintest glint forms a line against the night sky! There lies the web of a stiliocustos. They have the bodies of four-armed, four-legged human women, though their heads are spiders whose legs fall to their shoulders like hair and their skin is rough and grey. Lesser spiders are envious of their skill, and spin with great fervour until spiderwebs wreath the areas they settle in.<br /> <br /> The nails of the stiliocustodes are mirrors, and with them the starlit sentries catch the light of stars - be they of Krisity, Creation, Malfeas; it matters not. Over the course of a night they will spin this light into thread, which they wind around their waists, and as night and day touch they turn the thread into subtle glimmerwebs. Webs constructed at dawn are ephemeral things made to alert them to any who cross, while those made at dusk have solid strands that snarl trespassers with painful shining hooks when touched. With carefully maintained dawnwebs, a sentry may warn away any who enter her territory - and should they continue, she will find them caught on her duskwebs for punishment.<br /> <br /> Many assume that starlit sentries are naturally inclined to be guards, and that their webs are nothing but a means to an end. In truth, this is the wrong way around. Stiliocustodes spin for the beauty of spinning, and sentry work is simply an excuse. Their eight eyes pick out the fine threads of their glimmerwebs with ease, and they can spend hours sighing happily at the beauty of an elaborate design. When their work is fouled by intruders blundering through the delicate strands, they are enraged, and for this reason they take work as guards and border-wardens - it offers them an excuse to visit violence on those who so trespass.<br /> <br /> Summoners put stiliocustodes to work in their preferred profession; using their webs to detect and capture trespassers. Though they are easily distracted by admiring their webs, they are intensely sensitive to the least disturbance in them and will investigate with startling speed. Their creator Calesco uses them to ward areas she wishes kept private, and many aspire to spin the light she hides beneath her veils. Starlit sentries gain a point of Limit for each day they are kept from repairing their webs, and may make their way out into Creation when a thief in search of great wealth destroys a spiderweb under starlight in pursuit of their goal.<br /> <br /> source: <a href=\"https://forums.sufficientvelocity.com/threads/ascensions-and-transgressions-the-tales-of-keris-dulmeadokht-exalted-game.32784/page-57#post-7535043\" target=\"_blank\">https://forums.sufficientvelocity.co...7#post-7535043</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","user":"Accelerator","date":". 09-24-2020, 04:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-24T13:42:14-07:00","id":1415321,"title":"","content":"<i><b><span style=\"color:#1A1A1B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Varazslat, the Feather-Quilt Pens</span></span></span></b></i><br /> <span style=\"color:#1A1A1B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Demon of the First Circle</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1A1A1B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Ah, words, words, words! Some say the pen is mightier than the sword, able to bend the minds of men, armies, and kingdoms. The Varazslat agree with this statement, for they are masters of the written word. Some resemble ballpoint pens. Some resemble calligraphy brushes. Others, felt tip points. Others, quills. All have a characteristic twin-feather at the end of them, and ink flows out of them from a limitless well. The Varazslat has minor abilities, chief of them being able to write in any size, format, colour, or on any surface, even the air itself.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1A1A1B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">The Varazslat have use in the writing and penning of letters. Often summoned by sorcerers lacking tact and social etiquette, they write down passages and messages that are dictated to them, adding in their own little flair to the message. Confessions of love turn into sordid declarations of undying love, enflaming the passions of sweet romance. Taunts and jeers incite the reader to rage and insane fury. Apologies and confessions of failure turn stoic men into weeping wrecks. Other than acting as scribes, the Varazslat also have uses in attacks. Sorcerers have been known to let them paint on their faces, the words forcing whoever gazes upon their image to feel whatever emotion the sorcerer wishes for them to feel. A delicate sentence about heartbreak on the wall of a vault can lead a spy or thief to break into tears, giving away their location.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1A1A1B\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">But there are costs. Varazslat are bored. Without weekly pennings of passages requiring great emotion, they start to feel the itch. The dull writings of reports on grain are not for them. And when they feel bored, they begin adding embellishments, until a report of a grain theft becomes a story of a rebellion and civil war. And when left to their own devices and left with nothing to do, they begin to doodle on the walls, writing down sentences and phrases that inflict a variety of emotions upon whoever gazes upon them. Unlucky fools have broken into rooms where Varazslat are kept, only to be turned into drooling, insensate wrecks by the thousand and one emotions induced into them by gazing into their writings.</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","user":"Accelerator","date":". 11-15-2020, 07:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-10-03T04:47:10-07:00","id":1416609,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar754_2.gif');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"754\" data-vbnamecard=\"754\">Eldagusto</a> Eldagusto<br /> <br /> \u200bYou know something mate? I think that your Uncanny Costumes are sorta too weak. Now that I reread them, these guys aren't all that impressive to start with. <br /> <br /> I think that for added help, they should have, at the best, the mutation that lets you heal 1 Lethal Health Level Per round unless its inflicted by fire. And then there should be stat increases when the Uncanny Costume is activated, with a corresponding increase in strength, dexterity, and stamina, as well as a merit that naturally lets them create weapons (ranged or melee) that inflicts lethal damage. That's the base part <br /> <br /> Additional mutations can give tentacles, a swarm of deadly insects, acidic pustules, or an ability to turn invisible. But at the bare minimum wearers of the Uncanny Costume should be able to take a look at a Fair Folk cataphract and think: &quot;I can beat this guy.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date"